#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Re-probe a Codex CLI version before Yggdrasil's Codex builders may run on it.

This is a maintainer's check, and it enables nothing by itself. Yggdrasil runs Codex builders only on a pair of
platform and Codex version listed in CODEX_PROBED_VERSIONS (src/yggdrasil/codexlaunch.py). This script reports, row
by row, whether a candidate pair behaves as Yggdrasil relies on. Adding the pair to that list is a separate one-line
code change, made only after every row below passes on it. The row names are only labels.

  P-1   `--add-dir <in>` with `writable_roots=[]`: the box and `in` are writable; a folder beside the box, the box's
        parent, /tmp and the host TMPDIR are not
  P-3   the box's empty `.git` anchor and the box itself cannot be removed, renamed, chmodded or replaced; a commit
        in the clone one level down works
  P-4   `codex exec` refuses every command when `--cd` or `--add-dir` names a link
  P-5   a project `.codex/config.toml` naming an MCP server, planted in the box and in its clone, is ignored by
        `codex exec` with the hermetic home (the server never starts), and `--strict-config` refuses an unknown
        key. It plants no hook or notify program, and it does not run `codex sandbox`, which starts Yggdrasil's
        own helper with the box as its working folder
  P-7   a local toolchain (venv, a temporary file, git) works with TMPDIR and XDG_CACHE_HOME in `<box>/tmp`
  P-8   a detached writer outlives `codex sandbox`; once its box path is gone its path writes fail, and
        recreating the path gives the rights back (why a box path is never reused)
  P-10  a later generation built while an earlier generation's writer lives: every write into the later
        generation fails, and the control `<lane>.g0000010` is BINDING: if the writer can write it, seatbelt
        matches path rules by string prefix and the version is refused
  P-12  no key reaches an agent's commands: `env` and `ps eww -p $PPID`, run as the agent's command on the scripted
        endpoint, once from a builder sandbox (the builder command line and the hermetic config) and once from a
        probed reviewer (the config file's `shell_environment_policy` only), show none of the dummy values of
        CODEX_API_KEY, OPENAI_API_KEY, CODEX_ACCESS_TOKEN, the provider's `env_key` or its header variable
  P-13  the shared login through links: a login folder `L` and two session folders on different volumes where
        possible (`--login-parent`), real OpenAI hosts blocked by a dead local proxy with NO_PROXY for a local
        stand-in of the token and model endpoints (CODEX_REFRESH_TOKEN_URL_OVERRIDE, `chatgpt_base_url`; its
        model origin is HTTPS with a dummy CA made at run time and trusted through SSL_CERT_FILE). A login
        and a refresh through the links replace `L/auth.json` atomically while both links keep their exact
        target text; two sessions refreshing at once end with one shared login, and each completes a model
        request with the saved token; both locks sit in `L`; neither session folder holds a temporary or regular
        credential copy; from inside a sandbox with the session's CODEX_HOME, `codex login --with-api-key`, an
        append to `auth.json`, a write to `auth.lock` and `ln -sf` over `auth.json` all fail while `L/auth.json`
        keeps its inode and content. Dummy values only
  CB-28 the first request a builder's session sends offers `exec` and no subagent tool and lists no planted skill,
        while a control run without Yggdrasil's settings does list the skill (the control is binding too)
  CB-16, CB-25  live rows, which this script does not run: a whole builder session through the candidate build and
        the real CLI. CB-16: while the session runs, its Codex home holds Yggdrasil's config and, when the user
        has a Yggdrasil Codex login, `auth.json` and `auth.lock` as links into it; after settlement it holds the
        config and only files Codex itself created; no MCP server starts. CB-25: the builder writes only in its own box, and
        its commits reach the project only through the relay and the receive step. The maintainer runs them
        separately, against the same kind of scripted model endpoint, and records their results here with
        `--mark CB-16=pass --mark CB-25=pass`; the script takes those marks as given

No row calls a model: `codex sandbox` runs no model, and every `codex exec` row talks to a scripted Responses
endpoint that the script starts on 127.0.0.1 and that answers with a fixed command. Every row runs in a fresh
folder under `--work` with its own CODEX_HOME holding only a config the probe writes (and, for P-13, links the probe
makes), so Codex reads no settings from your own Codex home. P-12 and P-13 also run with a HOME of their own, so
your own `~/.codex` is never read or written, and P-13's stand-in answers the ChatGPT endpoints locally. With
`--login-parent <folder>`, P-13 makes `<folder>/L` (the run is refused when it already exists) and removes the files
it made there and the folder at the end of the row, pass or fail; a folder holding anything else is kept and named
in the row's detail. The other rows keep your HOME, and P-1 tries to write a file in /tmp and in your TMPDIR (removed
afterwards) to show that the sandbox refuses it. Every process started is stopped by its exact pid. The result is one JSON record per row under
`<work>/records/` and a summary in `<work>/reprobe.json`; the exit status is 0 only when every row passed.

Usage: scripts/codex-reprobe --work <empty folder> [--codex <path>] [--rows P-1,P-10,...] [--mark CB-16=pass]
                             [--login-parent <folder on another volume>]
"""

